.TH "ELEMENT.POINTERLEAVE_EVENT" "3JS" "July 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.pointerleave_event \- Element: pointerleave event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]pointerleave\f[R] event is fired when a pointing device is
moved out of the hit test boundaries of an element.
For pen devices, this event is fired when the stylus leaves the hover
range detectable by the digitizer.
Otherwise, \f[CR]pointerleave\f[R] works the same as
\f[CR]mouseleave\f[R], and are dispatched at the same time.
They are also dispatched at the same time as \f[CR]mouseout\f[R] and
\f[CR]pointerout\f[R] events, if appropriate.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqpointerleave\(dq, (event) => { })

onpointerleave = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]PointerEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Using \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.addEventListener(\(dqpointerleave\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer left element\(dq);
});
.EE
.PP
Using the \f[CR]onpointerleave\f[R] event handler property:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.onpointerleave = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer left element\(dq);
};
.EE
